          </h2>

          <p>
            If there is one rule in home espresso, it is that the grinder matters at least as much as the machine. Espresso extracts in under half a minute through a finely packed puck, so tiny changes in grind size change the flavour dramatically. Coffee ground in advance loses aroma within minutes and cannot be adjusted when a new bag of beans behaves differently.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why we put a grinder-built-in machine at the top for most buyers. The Barista Express and Barista Pro include burr grinders tuned for espresso, which means a new owner can adjust grind size from day one. The alternative, buying a machine like the Bambino Plus or Gaggia Classic plus a separate espresso grinder, gives more flexibility and usually a better grinder, but it costs more and takes more counter space.
          </p>

          <p>
            Avoid blade grinders for espresso. They chop rather than grind, producing a mix of dust and chunks that extracts unevenly. Even a modest burr grinder designed for espresso will make a bigger difference to your shots than upgrading the machine.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you buy a machine without a grinder, plan to spend a meaningful share of your budget on the grinder. A useful rule of thumb many enthusiasts follow is to never pair an expensive machine with a cheap grinder; the grinder sets the ceiling.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-100 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8 not-prose">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#1E90FF] mb-2">The Grinder We Would Pair</p>
            <p class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Baratza Encore ESP</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
              If you buy the Bambino, Bambino Plus, Gaggia, Flair or Stilosa, this is the grinder to start with. Retailers list 40mm conical steel burrs and a 40-step dial where settings 1 to 20 are fine micro-steps for espresso and 21 to 40 cover filter coffee, plus a dosing cup that fits both 54 mm and 58 mm portafilters. Seattle Coffee Gear and Cliff &amp; Pebble list it at $199.95, and deal trackers have recorded it at $159.95 on Amazon during sales.
            </p>
